            <ArticleTitle>Positive Captopril Renography Without Renal Artery Stenosis but a Renal Cell Carcinoma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>857-859</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/RLU.0000000000001833</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A positive captopril renography indicates that patient's hypertension is renin dependent, most commonly caused by renal artery stenosis. The authors reported a case of positive captopril renography; however, CT demonstrated that renal arteries were intact, but there was a huge chromophobe renal cell carcinoma. Renin-dependent hypertension was relieved soon after nephrectomy. It is an uncommon cause of positive captopril renography.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
